 "text": "**Starling Bank’s debit card is the strongest all-round choice for most UK residents who want genuinely fee-free spending and cash withdrawals abroad.**\n\n### Top debit cards (current accounts)\nThese charge **no foreign transaction fees** on card purchases and pass through a competitive Mastercard (or equivalent) rate with no bank markup:\n\n| Provider | Card spending abroad | ATM withdrawals abroad | Key notes |\n|----------|----------------------|------------------------|-----------|\n| **Starling** | None | None from Starling (no monthly cap) | Daily limit £300 (max 6 withdrawals). Excellent customer scores. Free account, strong app, FSCS-protected. |\n| **Chase UK** | None | None up to £500/day and £1,500/calendar month | Fee applies beyond the monthly limit. Cashback is UK-only (not on overseas spending). App-only. |\n| **First Direct** | None | None (up to £500/day) | Scrapped fees in 2023. Strong service reputation; often runs switching incentives. Mastercard rate. |\n\n**Monzo** is fee-free for card spending but charges 3% on ATM withdrawals above a monthly allowance (typically £200 outside the EEA, with higher/unlimited allowances in the EEA if it is your main account). Revolut’s free plan has monthly spending limits and a weekend markup, so it is less straightforward.\n\nWhich? ranks Starling highest among truly fee-free options by customer score, followed by First Direct and Chase.\n\n### Strong credit card options (for purchases)\nThese also charge **0% foreign transaction fees** and can add Section 75 protection on purchases of £100–£30,000:\n\n- **Lloyds Bank Ultra** — 0% fees on foreign purchases and no Lloyds cash-withdrawal fee. 1% cashback in the first year (then 0.25%). Interest starts immediately on cash advances, so use a debit card for ATMs. Relatively low representative APR. Often highlighted as a Which? Best Buy.\n- **Barclaycard Rewards** — 0% fees on purchases and cash withdrawals. Unusually, no interest on overseas cash withdrawals if you clear the balance in full by the due date. 0.25% cashback. Higher APR.\n\n### Practical tips\n- Always choose to be charged in the **local currency**, not GBP. Selecting pounds triggers dynamic currency conversion (DCC), which usually gives a worse rate.\n- Overseas ATM operators can still add their own fee even if your bank charges nothing—check the screen and cancel if needed.\n- Carry a backup card. Limits, eligibility, and terms can change, so confirm the latest details with the provider before you travel.\n\n**Bottom line:** Open a **Starling** account if you want the simplest fee-free option for both spending and cash with no monthly ATM cap. Add **Chase** or **First Direct** if you prefer their other features, and consider the **Lloyds Ultra** credit card if you want cashback plus purchase protection on holiday spending.",
